Run frame-by-frame animation work on the UI thread by using a serializable native-driver animation or a UI-thread worklet, instead of updating React state on every frame. React Native's `Animated` API can set `useNativeDriver: true`; this avoids JS involvement after the animation starts, but it is limited mainly to non-layout properties such as `opacity` and `transform`. For richer gestures and layout work, Reanimated uses **shared values** and **worklets** that execute on the UI thread. Keep business logic and network work on JS, cross the boundary deliberately, and profile before claiming a win.

```tsx caption="Native-driver animation for opacity and transform"
const opacity = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;
useEffect(() => {
  Animated.timing(opacity, { toValue: 1, duration: 250, useNativeDriver: true }).start();
}, [opacity]);
return <Animated.View style={{ opacity, transform: [{ scale: opacity }] }} />;
```

answer.es:
Corré el trabajo de cada frame en el hilo de UI usando una animación serializable con native driver o un worklet de UI, en vez de actualizar estado de React en cada frame. La API `Animated` de React Native acepta `useNativeDriver: true`; después de arrancar, la animación no depende de JS, pero está limitada principalmente a propiedades que no son de layout, como `opacity` y `transform`. Para gestos más ricos y trabajo de layout, Reanimated usa **shared values** y **worklets** que se ejecutan en el hilo de UI. Dejá la lógica de negocio y la red en JS, cruzá la frontera de forma deliberada y perfilá antes de afirmar que mejoraste el rendimiento.

```tsx caption="Animación con native driver para opacidad y transform"
const opacity = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;
useEffect(() => {
  Animated.timing(opacity, { toValue: 1, duration: 250, useNativeDriver: true }).start();
}, [opacity]);
return <Animated.View style={{ opacity, transform: [{ scale: opacity }] }} />;
```

